        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90003</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Certainly! Below is a concise scientific abstract describing real-world use of oral selective estrogen receptor degraders in patients with endocrine-resistant metastatic breast cancer. <bold>Methods: </bold>Electronic records from three oncology centers were reviewed. Eligible patients had received an oral selective estrogen receptor degrader after progression on at least one endocrine regimen. Treatment duration and reasons for discontinuation were summarized. <bold>Results: </bold>The cohort included 126 patients. Median treatment duration was 5.8 months. Disease progression accounted for 79% of discontinuations, adverse events for 13%, and patient preference for 5%. Dose reduction occurred in 18% of patients. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Real-world treatment duration was consistent with use in a heavily pretreated population. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
